 last night and have persisted to this morning. He also has a past medical history": "Based on the provided information, the patient's symptoms and laboratory results suggest a diagnosis of meningitis, specifically viral meningitis. The key findings that support this diagnosis include:\n\n1. Fever, nausea, and vomiting, which are common symptoms of meningitis. 2. Diminished mobility of the neck in all four directions, with flexion of the neck causing discomfort, which is indicative of neck stiffness, a classic sign of meningitis. 3. Elevated cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) cell count (175/mm^3), which is indicative of inflammation in the central nervous system. 4. Normal CSF glucose level (49 mg/dL), which is consistent with viral meningitis, as bacterial meningitis typically presents with low CSF glucose levels. 5. Elevated CSF protein level (55 mg/dL), which is indicative of inflammation in the central nervous system. The most alarming aspect of this case is the patient's neck stiffness, which is a classic sign of meningitis. This symptom suggests that the patient's condition is potentially life-threatening and requires prompt medical attention. The most noteworthy aspect of this case is the patient's request to keep the lights in the room turned off during the physical exam. This behavior is often seen in patients with meningitis, who may experience photophobia (sensitivity to light) due to inflammation in the central nervous system.."}, "option_analyses": {"**\n\nA 32-year-old man presents to the emergency department with fever, nausea, and vomiting. He has a past medical history": "Based on the provided information, the patient's symptoms and laboratory results suggest a diagnosis of viral meningitis. The key findings that support this diagnosis include:\n\n1. Fever, nausea, and vomiting, which are common symptoms of meningitis.\n2. Diminished mobility of the neck in all four directions, with flexion of the neck causing discomfort, which is indicative of neck stiffne